{
  "id": "ADR-JEV-0006",
  "title": "Quality trees and their answers are an RDF graph",
  "status": "Draft",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "created": "2026-11-07",
  "last_updated": "2026-11-08",
  "owner": "Rand Lee",
  "supersedes": null,
  "superseded_by": null,
  "decision_date": "2026-11-07",
  "context": {
    "text": "A quality tree is a graph: waves, questions, options and rules, with edges\nbetween them. As JSON, a question shared by two trees or two waves is\ncopied, list position carries rule priority, and every structural check is\ncode judge-jev maintains. Answers live in log files apart from the questions\nthat produced them, so comparing thousands of question variations means\nscraping logs. The problem is how to represent trees and answers so that\nquestions are shared, structure is checked by existing tools, and every\nverdict can be traced through its answers to the exact question and option.",
    "background": "",
    "problem_statement": ""
  },
  "decision": {
    "text": "Quality trees are written in Turtle (TTL) against a judge-jev ontology:\nwaves, questions, options and rules are resources linked by edges, and a\nquestion is one resource with an IRI that any wave may use. Order is a\n`rank` property: rules are tried by rank and the reason lists questions by\nrank; questions within a wave are otherwise unordered. SHACL shapes over the\nontology validate the graph and its edges (each rule's choices are options\nof its question, each `goto` names a node, no cycle, a final rule without\nconditions). Pydantic models (ADR-JEV-0005) validate each node's body,\nincluding the per-wave JSON compiled from the graph and sent to Jev. Runs,\nanswers, fired rules and verdicts are recorded as triples linked to the\nquestions, options and Raptor records they concern, in an Oxigraph store\n(`pyoxigraph` in judge-jev). A tree's digest is the SHA-256 of its\ncanonical form (RDFC-1.0).\n\nThe vocabulary, prefix `jj: <urn:judge-jev:ontology#>`:\n\n- Tree: `jj:Tree` (`jj:start`, `jj:node`, `jj:kind`), `jj:Wave`\n  (`jj:asks` a `jj:Question`, `jj:rule`, `jj:per`, `jj:statements`,\n  `jj:modal`), `jj:Leaf` (`jj:verdict`).\n- Question: `jj:Question` (`jj:instructions`, `jj:option` a `jj:Option`,\n  `jj:rank`), `jj:Option` (`jj:label`, `jj:criterion`).\n- Rule: `jj:BranchRule` (`jj:goto`) and `jj:VerdictRule` (`jj:verdict`),\n  each with `jj:rank` and `jj:whenAll` / `jj:whenAny` a `jj:Condition`\n  (`jj:question`, `jj:choice` an `jj:Option`).\n- Run: `jj:Run` (`jj:tree`, `jj:treeDigest`, `jj:record` the Raptor\n  record's IRI), `jj:WaveRun` (`jj:inRun`, `jj:node`, `jj:requestId`,\n  `jj:statement`, `jj:fired` a rule), `jj:Answer` (`jj:ofWaveRun`,\n  `jj:toQuestion`, `jj:chose` an `jj:Option`, `jj:confidence`,\n  `jj:probability` a `jj:OptionProbability` with `jj:option` and `jj:p`),\n  `jj:verdictValue` on a run.",
    "chosen_approach": "",
    "key_principles": []
  },
  "rationale": {
    "text": "Validation of the graph moves to SHACL and of node bodies to pydantic, so\njudge-jev's own checking code shrinks to the compile step. One graph of\nrecords, questions and answers makes any verdict traceable and any two\nquestion variations comparable with one query.",
    "benefits": [],
    "trade_offs": []
  },
  "consequences": {
    "text": "",
    "positive": [
      "A question is written once and used by IRI.",
      "A query over the graph compares question variations across runs.",
      "The graph renders in a graph viewer for authoring and diagnosis.",
      "The Rust Jev tool in sc-agent can process the same ontology with\nOxigraph."
    ],
    "negative": [
      "`pyoxigraph` and a SHACL validator become dependencies.",
      "Each wave needs a compile step from the graph to Jev's JSON request.",
      "Tree authors write Turtle instead of JSON."
    ],
    "neutral": []
  },
  "alternatives": {
    "text": "",
    "groups": [
      {
        "name": "JSON trees with references to shared question files",
        "description": "Keep JSON; a wave lists `$ref`s that judge-jev resolves.",
        "pros": [],
        "cons": [],
        "why_rejected": "It removes duplication only; structure checks stay in\njudge-jev's code, and answers stay apart from questions."
      },
      {
        "name": "JSON trees as today",
        "description": "Keep one JSON file per tree with inline questions.",
        "pros": [],
        "cons": [],
        "why_rejected": "Questions are copied, priority is list position, and\nanswers cannot be joined to questions without scraping logs."
      }
    ]
  },
