{
  "id": "ADR-JEV-0007",
  "title": "The seam to sc-agent's Jev tool",
  "status": "Draft",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "created": "2026-11-07",
  "last_updated": "2026-11-08",
  "owner": "Rand Lee",
  "supersedes": null,
  "superseded_by": null,
  "decision_date": "2026-11-07",
  "context": {
    "text": "sc-agent's Rust Jev tool will replace judge-jev's Python Jev core\n(ADR-JEV-0001). Its first version will not include Oxigraph, though a later\none may. Quality trees and their answers become an RDF graph\n(ADR-JEV-0006). The problem is where the boundary lies, so that judge-jev\nworks with a Jev tool that has no graph store today, and one with Oxigraph\nlater takes over graph work without judge-jev changing its callers.",
    "background": "",
    "problem_statement": ""
  },
  "decision": {
    "text": "judge-jev has two ports:\n\n- **Transport:** sends the request lines of one wave and returns their\n  result lines. Its contract is the existing batch request and result\n  schemas (`assets/schemas/jev-batch-request.schema.json`,\n  `jev-batch-result.schema.json`), unchanged. The Python core implements it\n  now; sc-agent's Jev tool implements it later.\n- **Graph store:** loads a tree, validates it with SHACL, compiles a wave\n  to its JSON questions, records a run's triples and answers queries.\n  judge-jev implements it now with `pyoxigraph` and a SHACL validator; a\n  Jev tool with Oxigraph may implement it later.\n\nOnly JSON crosses the transport. The result lines in the sc-observability\nlog are the record of a run; a run's triples are a projection of those\nlines and the tree, so they can be rebuilt from the log at any time. The\ntree walk (which wave next, which verdict) uses only the compiled waves and\nthe answers, so it runs on either side of the seam.",
    "chosen_approach": "",
    "key_principles": []
  },
  "rationale": {
    "text": "A Jev tool without a graph store needs only the JSON contract it already\nhas. Because the triples are rebuilt from the log, no run is lost when the\nstore moves to the other side of the seam.",
    "benefits": [],
    "trade_offs": []
  },
  "consequences": {
    "text": "",
    "positive": [
      "sc-agent's first Jev tool replaces the Python core with no graph work.",
      "Moving the graph store into sc-agent changes the port's implementation,\nnot its callers."
    ],
    "negative": [
      "Until sc-agent hosts the store, judge-jev carries the graph dependencies.",
      "The projection from result lines to triples must stay in step with the\nontology."
    ],
    "neutral": []
  },
  "alternatives": {
    "text": "",
    "groups": [
      {
        "name": "sc-agent's Jev tool reads trees and writes triples",
        "description": "Send the TTL tree and the record; the tool walks the tree\nand stores the triples.",
        "pros": [],
        "cons": [],
        "why_rejected": "Its first version has no graph store, so judge-jev would\nwait for it."
      },
      {
        "name": "Triples as the only record of a run",
        "description": "Write runs to the graph store instead of the log.",
        "pros": [],
        "cons": [],
        "why_rejected": "A tool without a store could not record a run, and a lost\nstore loses the runs."
      }
    ]
  },
